explanation:
The change adds three new class-level regexes to `Lexer`: `_raw_start_re = r"%s\s+(?:comment|verbatim) "` and the two end regexes using `[^\S
]+endcomment`/`[^\S
]+endverbatim`. The `+` quantifiers require at least one space between `{%` and the tag name. Django's general `tag_re` (`{%.*?%}`) and the old code (`content = token_string[2:-2].strip()` then `content[:9] in ("verbatim", "verbatim ")`) tolerated `{%verbatim%}` with no spaces, so this is a regression introduced by this diff. With the new code, `{%verbatim%}` does not trigger the raw-aware splitter, so the block's contents are lexed as real tags/variables (and `create_token` no longer has any verbatim handling), producing wrong output or a TemplateSyntaxError; and `{% verbatim %}...{%endverbatim%}` never finds its closing tag. The fix simply changes `\s+` and `[^\S
]+` to `\s*` and `[^\S
]*`, exactly as the ground truth says. A careful reviewer comparing the new regexes with the permissive `tag_re`/`.strip()` handling they replace could spot this from the diff alone.
